\\n\\n1\\n\\nUnder matrix multiplication, the permutation matrices of size n×n comprise a subgroup of the\\n\\ngeneral linear group. This is a property that does not extend at all, within the class of ASMs. It is\\n\\nshown in [7] that if a pair of nonsingular ASMs are inverses of each other, then they are permuta-\\n\\ntion matrices. It follows that the only subgroups of a general linear group whose elements are all\\n\\nASMs are groups of permutation matrices. However, it is possible for a set of singular ASMs to\\n\\nform a group under matrix multiplication, with an identity element that is an idempotent ASM.\\n\\nThe purpose of this article is to initiate an exploration of this phenomenon. We present examples\\n\\nand constructions, and propose some questions for further investigations. We show in particular\\n\\nthat the symmetric group S n, and hence any group of order n, can be realized in multiple ways\\n\\nas multiplicative groups of singular ASMs.\\n\\n2 Groups of Singular ASMs and Associated Linear Groups\\n\\nalso idempotent. Moreover, for every A∈G\\n\\nEA =A=AE =⇒(I n−T)A=A=A(I n−T) = A=⇒TA =AT =0.\\n\\nIt follows that rank(T)6 n−r. On the other hand the rank of T is at least n−r, since T+E=I n.\\n\\nThus T has rank n−r and its rowspace and columnspace are exactly the left and right nullspaces\\n\\nof E(and of every element of G).\\n\\nOur next theorem demonstrates a natural correspondence between singular groups and linear\\n\\ngroups.\\n\\nTheorem 2.2. Let G be a singular group of matrices in F n×n with identity E=I n−T, and let Γ=\\n\\n{X+T:X∈G}. Then Γ is a linear group, isomorphic to G via the bijection X←→ X+T.\\n\\nProof. Let A,B∈G. Then (A+T)(B+T) = AB +T B +AT +T 2=AB +T. Since E+T=I n, it\\n\\nfollows that the mapping from G to Γ deﬁned by X→X+T is a homomorphism of groups, and\\n\\n2\\n\\nthat its image consists of matrices of rank n. It is clearly injective, so its image is isomorphic to\\n\\nG.\\n\\nWe remark that the isomorphism from Γ to G of Theorem 2.2 may be described as subtraction of T\\n\\nor as multiplication (on the left or right) by E. For every element Y of Γ we have Y−T=Y E =EY.\\n\\nA possible strategy for constructing a group G of singular ASMs is to start with a particular\\n\\nidempotent E∈SA n as the identity element of G, and try to identify a linear group that is a\\n\\ncandidate for Γ. This would require that every element of Γ commutes with E, but we may ﬁrst\\n\\nsuppose that Φ is a linear group with the property that EΦ ={EH :H∈Φ}is a set of ASMs.\\n\\nFor EΦ to be a group requires that EH and E have the same row space for every H∈Φ(that\\n\\nthey have the same column space is automatic, since each H∈Φ is nonsingular). Candidates\\n\\nfor Φ are groups of matrices that preserve the alternating sign structure when multiplying E on\\n\\nthe right, and also preserve the rowspace of E. These are substantial restrictions, and the obvious\\n\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nProof of the refined alternating sign matrix conjecture - MaRDI portal (https://portal.mardi4nfdi.de/w/index.php?redirect=no&title=Publication%3A1372193)\\nciteturn0search5 [wordlim: 200] Published: 29.0 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; # Proof of the refined alternating sign matrix conjecture ...   * Extreme diagonally and antidiagonally symmetric alternating sign matrices of odd order ...   * Short proof of the ASM theorem avoiding the six-vertex model ...   * Groups of singular alternating sign matrices\\n\\nMathematics Subject Classification ID\\n\\nExact enumeration problems, generating functions (05A15) (q)-calculus and related topics (05A30) Combinatorial aspects of matrices (incidence, Hadamard, etc.) (05B20)\\n\\nAbstract: Mills, Robbins, and Rumsey conjectured, and Zeilberger proved, that the number of alternating sign matrices of order {\\\\displaystyle n} equals {\\\\displaystyle A(n):=1!4!7!\\\\mathrm{...}(3n-2)!overn!(n+1)!\\\\mathrm{...}(2n-1)!